:!
! [command line]
  Runs C:\sys\ROMSHELL.COM, the shell, without C:\STARTUP.CMD. ! and RUN are
  the same command.
  Alone, it gives the shell's prompt, and the shell's END comes back to DB.
  With a command line, the shell runs that line, and the script it starts,
  then comes back. The table in use and the current record are kept.
  ! DIR *.DB
:?
? [expression [, expression ...]]
  Prints the values with a space between them, then ends the line. ? alone
  prints an empty line. A logical prints as .T. or .F..
  ? NAME, AGE * 2
:@
@ row, column [SAY expression] [GET name [PICTURE text]]
@ row, column TO row, column [DOUBLE]
@ row, column PROMPT text
  Puts the cursor at row and column and prints the SAY value there with no
  line end. Row and column count from 0, and are 0 to 255. With neither SAY
  nor GET it blanks the row from column to its end. The line is checked
  whole before the cursor moves.
  GET adds a pending GET for READ and shows it at once: after the SAY value
  and a space, or at row and column. name is a field of the current record,
  else a memory variable that exists. A GET on a field past the last record
  gives Record out of range.
  The width of a GET is the PICTURE's length, else the field's length, the
  text variable's length, 10 or more for a number variable, 1 for a
  logical. A number is right-aligned, and a value is cut to the width. A GET
  must fit on its row.
  At most 32 GETs are pending. Each takes twice its width plus 4 bytes of a
  2048-byte pool. Past either limit @ gives Too many GETs.
  @ ... TO draws a box from the first corner, the top left, to the second,
  the bottom right, as BOX does.
  @ ... PROMPT prints text there and adds it to the prompts MENU TO picks
  from. At most 16 prompts.
  HELPDB PICTURE gives the PICTURE characters, HELPDB READ the editing.
  @ 2, 0 SAY "Name" GET NAME PICTURE "!XXXXXXXXX"
  @ 1, 10 TO 5, 40 DOUBLE
:ACCEPT
ACCEPT [prompt] TO name
  Prints prompt, a text, then stores the line typed as text in the memory
  variable name. name is made when it is new.
  ACCEPT "Name? " TO WHO
:APPEND
APPEND [BLANK]
  APPEND adds records. For each it prints "Record n" and asks for each field
  by name. A C or D field takes the answer cut to its length, an N field a
  number expression, an L field T, Y, F or N. A wrong answer prints its
  error and is asked again. An empty answer leaves the field blank.
  An empty answer to the first field ends APPEND, and that record is not
  added.
  APPEND BLANK adds one blank record.
  The last record added is current.
:BOF
BOF()
  .T. when SKIP with a negative count has stopped at the first record of
  the order, and in a table with no records. Else .F.. With no table in use
  it is .F..
:BOX
BOX row, column, width, height [DOUBLE]
  Draws a box of single lines, or double lines with DOUBLE, its top left
  corner at row and column, from 0. The inside is cleared to spaces. width
  and height are at least 2. The box takes the current colors.
  A box that ends in the screen's last column moves the cursor to the next
  row, and on the last row that scrolls the screen.
  BOX 2, 10, 30, 6 DOUBLE
:CANCEL
CANCEL
  Ends every command file running. DB goes back to the dot prompt. At the
  prompt CANCEL does nothing.
:CHANGING
APPEND  EDIT  REPLACE  DELETE  RECALL  PACK  ZAP
  The commands that add and change records. A change to a record is written
  to the table at once. DELETE only marks a record; PACK removes the marked
  records. ZAP removes every record.
  HELPDB name gives each command.
:CLEAR
CLEAR [GETS]
  CLEAR blanks the screen, puts the cursor at row 0, column 0, and drops the
  pending GETs. CLEAR GETS only drops the pending GETs.
:CLS
CLS
  Blanks the screen as CLEAR does, and keeps the pending GETs.
:CONTINUE
CONTINUE
  Goes on with the last LOCATE, from the record after the current one in the
  order, over what is left of the LOCATE's scope. Prints "Record n", or "End
  of LOCATE" at the scope's end. Without a LOCATE it gives No LOCATE active.
  USE, CREATE and SELECT end the LOCATE.
:COPY
COPY TO path
  Makes the table path, with .DB added when it has no extension, with the
  fields of the table in use. Copies to it, in the order, each record that
  is not deleted and meets SET FILTER, and prints "n records copied". The
  place past the last record is then current.
  A file of that path is emptied. The path of the table of either work
  area gives Table in use. Another path to the table in use, such as
  .\PEOPLE.DB for PEOPLE.DB, empties it.
  COPY TO OLDPEOPLE
:COUNT
COUNT [scope] [TO name]
  Prints how many records of the scope meet its FOR condition, as "n
  records". The scope is every record when left out. TO stores the count in
  the memory variable name.
  COUNT FOR AGE > 30 TO OLDER
:CREATE
CREATE path
  Makes a new table, path with .DB added when it has no extension. A file of
  that path is emptied.
  CREATE asks for the fields, one a line, as name,type,length,decimals. An
  empty line ends the fields. A table has at most 32 fields. A wrong line
  prints its error and is asked again.
  A name is 1 to 10 letters, digits and _, starting with a letter. Two
  fields of one name give Duplicate field.
  C is text, length 1 to 255. N is a number, length 1 to 10, then the
  decimals, 0 when left out. With decimals the length is at least decimals
  plus 2. L is a logical, typed as name,L. D is a date as text, typed as
  name,D, length 8.
  The new table is in use, with no records. With no fields no table is made.
  When the table cannot be made, no table is in use.
  CREATE PEOPLE
  NAME,C,20
  AGE,N,3
  PAID,L
:DATE
DATE()
  The date of the clock, as text MM/DD/YY.
:DB
DB [file]
  DB is a database at a dot prompt, after dBASE II. XBASE.COM starts it. DB
  file runs the command file file first, as DO does. QUIT ends it.
  DB prints the prompt ". " and runs each line typed. A command word may be
  cut to its first 4 letters or more, as DISP STRU for DISPLAY STRUCTURE.
  Small letters are the same as capitals.
  A table is a .DB file, an index a .IDX file, a command file a .PRG file.
  An error prints its message and stops the command. A command on a table
  with none in use gives No table in use. A line whose first word is no
  command gives Unknown command, unless it is name = value.
  DB keeps its memory variables in a RAM bank of their own. Without a free
  bank DB prints the error and ends.
  HELP [topic] at the prompt runs HELPDB. ! runs the shell.
  HELPDB alone lists the topics. HELPDB name prints a topic. The groups:
  TABLES, MOVING, SHOWING, CHANGING, INDEXES, VARIABLES, SCREENS, PROGRAMS,
  FUNCTIONS, OPERATORS, SCOPE, PICTURE, KEYS, EXPRESSIONS.
  . USE PEOPLE
  . LIST FOR AGE > 30
:DELETE
DELETE [scope]
  Marks each record of the scope that meets its FOR condition deleted, and
  prints "n records deleted". The scope is the current record when left
  out. LIST shows a deleted record with *. RECALL clears the mark, and PACK
  removes the deleted records.
  DELETE FOR AGE < 18
:DELETED
DELETED()
  .T. when the current record is marked deleted. Else .F.. Past the last
  record and with no table in use it is .F..
:DISPLAY
DISPLAY [scope] | DISPLAY STRUCTURE | DISPLAY MEMORY
  DISPLAY scope prints each record of the scope that meets its FOR
  condition, as LIST does. The scope is the current record when left out.
  DISPLAY STRUCTURE prints the table's path, its record count, and each
  field's number, name, type, length, and decimals for an N field. Total is
  the record's length, the delete flag included.
  DISPLAY MEMORY prints each memory variable: its name, its kind C, N or L,
  and its value, a text in quotes. Then it prints the count of variables
  and the bytes free.
  DISP STRU
:DO
DO file | DO WHILE condition
  DO file runs the command file, with .PRG added when it has no extension.
  Its lines run as if typed at the prompt. The end of the file, or RETURN,
  goes back to the caller. Command files nest 8 deep.
  DO WHILE runs the lines up to its ENDDO while condition is true. ENDDO
  goes back to the DO WHILE. When condition is false, the lines up to its
  ENDDO are skipped. At most 8 DO WHILEs run at once, over all the files.
  condition is a logical; any other value gives Type mismatch.
  At the prompt DO WHILE gives Only in a command file.
  More than 8 files or loops gives Too many DO levels.
  N = 1
  DO WHILE N <= 3
    ? N
    N = N + 1
  ENDDO
:EDIT
EDIT
  Prints "Record n", then each field of the current record by name with its
  value, and asks for a new value after ?. An empty answer keeps the value.
  A C or D field takes the answer cut to its length, an N field a number
  expression, an L field T, Y, F or N. A wrong answer prints its error and
  is asked again. Then the record is written.
  EDIT takes no record number; GO to the record first. Past the last record
  EDIT gives Record out of range.
:ELSE
ELSE
  Starts the lines of an IF that run when its condition is false. HELPDB IF
  gives IF.
:ENDDO
ENDDO
  Ends the lines of a DO WHILE, and goes back to the innermost DO WHILE
  running. Without one it does nothing. HELPDB DO gives DO WHILE.
:ENDIF
ENDIF
  Ends the lines of an IF. HELPDB IF gives IF.
:EOF
EOF()
  .T. when the place past the last record is current, as after LIST, SKIP
  past the last record, or a FIND with no match. Else .F.. With no table in
  use it is .F..
:EXPRESSIONS
12  -3.5  .5  "text"  'text'  .T. .F. .Y. .N.  field  name  P.name  S.name
  A value is a number, a text or a logical.
  A number is typed in decimal. A text is in " or '. .T. and .Y. are true,
  .F. and .N. false.
  A field name gives the field of the current record: an N field a number,
  0 when blank; an L field a logical, true for T or Y in either case; a C
  or D field text, with its padding spaces. Past the last record a field is
  blank.
  P.name and S.name give a field of the PRIMARY or SECONDARY area's table.
  Any other name is a memory variable. A field hides a memory variable of
  its name. Names are the same in small letters and capitals.
  The texts of one expression hold at most 254 characters, else Text too
  long. At most 16 operators and brackets wait at once, else Syntax error.
  Values of two kinds in one operation give Type mismatch. A name that is
  neither a field nor a variable gives Variable not found.
  HELPDB OPERATORS gives the operators, HELPDB FUNCTIONS the functions.
:FILE
FILE(path)
  .T. when a file or folder matches path, a text. Else .F..
  ? FILE("PEOPLE.DB")
:FIND
FIND key
  Makes current the first record of the order whose index key starts with
  key. key is the rest of the line, with the quotes around it taken off.
  With no match it prints "No find", and the place past the last record is
  current.
  FIND needs an index as the order. In the record order, or with a key
  longer than the index's key, it gives Invalid data.
  An N field enters the index key rewritten so that it sorts as a number.
  FIND compares key as typed.
  FIND Smith
:FUNCTIONS
RECNO EOF BOF DELETED DATE LEN UPPER TRIM VAL INT FILE REG STR SUBSTR MENU
  A function name is typed whole, with its ( right after it, no space
  between. An unknown name gives Unknown function. A wrong number of
  arguments gives Syntax error, and an argument of the wrong kind Type
  mismatch. A count or a place argument is 0 to 255, else Number out of
  range.
  HELPDB name gives each function, without the brackets.
:GET
GET name [PICTURE text]
  Part of @. HELPDB @ gives @ ... GET.
:GO
GO n | GO TOP | GO BOTTOM
  GO n makes record n current. n is the record's number in the file, 1 to
  the record count, else Record out of range. GO TOP makes the first record
  of the order current, GO BOTTOM the last. GO prints "Record n".
  GO TOP
:GOTO
GOTO n | GOTO TOP | GOTO BOTTOM
  The same as GO. HELPDB GO gives GO.
:HELP
HELP [topic]
  Runs C:\sys\HELPXBASE.COM on topic, then comes back to the prompt. HELP
  alone lists the topics.
  HELP REPLACE
:IF
IF condition
  In a command file, when condition is false, the lines up to this IF's
  ELSE or ENDIF are skipped. When it is true the lines run, and an ELSE
  skips the lines up to its ENDIF. IFs nest. An IF has at most one ELSE.
  condition is a logical; any other value gives Type mismatch.
  At the prompt IF gives Only in a command file, and ELSE and ENDIF do
  nothing.
  IF AGE > 30
    ? "Older"
  ELSE
    ? "Younger"
  ENDIF
:INDEX
INDEX ON key TO name [UNIQUE]
  Builds the index name, with .IDX added when it has no extension. key is 1
  to 4 field names joined by +. With UNIQUE the index refuses two records
  with one key. UNIQUE is typed whole.
  The open indexes close first. The new index is the only one open, and the
  order. INDEX prints "n records indexed", and the first record of the
  order is current.
  INDEX ON LAST + FIRST TO NAMES
:INDEXES
INDEX  SET INDEX  USE ... INDEX  FIND  REINDEX
  An index orders the records of a table by a key of 1 to 4 fields. The
  order is the first index USE or SET INDEX TO opens, or the index INDEX ON
  builds. With no index open it is the record order. A work area has at
  most 3 open indexes.
  GO TOP, GO BOTTOM, SKIP, FIND and the scopes ALL and NEXT follow the
  order. GO n and RECORD n take the record's number in the file.
  HELPDB name gives each of INDEX, SET, USE, FIND and REINDEX.
:INPUT
INPUT [prompt] TO name
  Prints prompt, a text, then stores the value of the expression typed in
  the memory variable name: a number, a text in quotes, or a logical. name
  is made when it is new.
  INPUT "Age? " TO AGE
:INT
INT(number)
  number cut to a whole number toward 0. INT(-2.7) is -2.
:KEYS
READ's keys
  A typed character types over the one at the cursor when the PICTURE's
  character in that place takes it. Else it is ignored.
  Left, Right, Home and End move the cursor. The cursor skips the
  PICTURE's fixed characters.
  Backspace moves left and puts a space. Delete puts a space at the cursor.
  Enter, Tab and Down go to the next GET. At the last GET they end READ and
  store the GETs.
  Up goes to the GET before.
  PgDn and Ctrl+W end READ and store the GETs.
  Esc ends READ and stores none.
:LEN
LEN(text)
  The number of characters of text. A C field counts its padding spaces.
  ? LEN(TRIM(NAME))
:LIST
LIST [scope]
  Prints each record of the scope that meets its FOR condition: its number,
  * when it is marked deleted, then its fields, a space before each. The
  scope is every record when left out, and leaves the place past the last
  record current.
  LIST FOR AGE > 30
:LOCATE
LOCATE [scope]
  Makes current the first record of the scope that meets its FOR condition,
  and prints "Record n". At the scope's end it prints "End of LOCATE". The
  scope is every record when left out. CONTINUE finds the next record.
  LOCATE FOR CITY = "Boston"
:MENU
MENU TO name | MENU(row, column, items)
  MENU TO shows the prompts of @ ... PROMPT, one picked at a time, and
  stores in the memory variable name the number of the one picked, from 1
  in the order they were made, or 0 for Esc. With no prompt it stores 0
  and waits for no key. The prompts are then dropped.
  MENU(row, column, items), the function, draws a box of single lines, its
  top left corner at row and column, with the items inside, one a row, and
  gives the number of the item picked, or 0 for Esc. items is a text, the
  items apart by ;, at most 16. The box is as wide as the widest item, and
  stays on the screen. The prompts of @ ... PROMPT are dropped.
  Up and Left go to the one before, Down and Right to the next, around the
  ends. Enter picks the one shown. A key picks the first whose first
  character it is, in either case.
  @ 5, 10 PROMPT "Add"
  @ 6, 10 PROMPT "Quit"
  MENU TO PICK
  ? MENU(2, 30, "Red;Green;Blue")
:MODIFY
MODIFY COMMAND file
  Runs C:\sys\EDIT.COM on file, with .PRG added when it has no extension.
  The tables, indexes and command files stay open. Each takes one of the
  files the editor can open.
  MODIFY COMMAND REPORT
:MOVING
GO  GOTO  SKIP  LOCATE  CONTINUE  FIND
  The commands that change the current record. Past the last record is a
  place of its own: EOF() is .T. there, and RECNO() is the record count
  plus 1.
  HELPDB name gives each command.
:OPERATORS
+ - * / ( ) = # < > <= >= $ .NOT. .AND. .OR.
  + - * / work on numbers. + joins two texts. A - before a value changes
  its sign.
  = # < > <= >= compare two numbers or two texts and give a logical. # is
  not equal. Two texts compare over the length of the right one: "ABC" =
  "AB" is .T., "AB" = "ABC" .F.. Logicals do not compare.
  a $ b is .T. when the text a is in the text b.
  .NOT. .AND. .OR. work on logicals.
  The order, first to last: - before a value, * /, + -, the compares,
  .NOT., .AND., .OR.. Brackets go first.
  A division by 0 gives Division by zero. A number too large gives Number
  out of range.
  ? "SMI" $ NAME .AND. AGE >= 21
:PACK
PACK
  Removes the deleted records and prints "n records left". The first record
  of the order is then current. A PACK that fails prints its error and
  leaves no table in use.
:PICTURE
PICTURE text
  The mask of a GET. Each character of text is one place of the GET:
  9 takes a digit.
  # takes a digit, a space, + - or a point.
  A takes a letter.
  L takes T, F, Y or N, made a capital.
  X takes any character.
  ! takes any character, a letter made a capital.
  Any other character is fixed: it shows in its place, and the cursor skips
  it. With no PICTURE the mask is all # for a number, L for a logical, else
  all X.
  @ 5, 0 GET PHONE PICTURE "(999) 999-9999"
:POP
POP
  Takes back the last PUSH: selects its work area and makes its record
  current, and BOF() is as it was. A record past the last of the table now
  in use is the place past the last. POP ends the LOCATE. Without a PUSH
  it gives Stack empty.
:PROGRAMS
DO IF ELSE ENDIF DO WHILE ENDDO RETURN CANCEL ACCEPT INPUT WAIT MODIFY !
  A command file is a text file with the extension .PRG, under 64 KB. DO
  runs it, and MODIFY COMMAND edits it. Its lines run as if typed at the
  prompt.
  A line ends in LF, and a CR is dropped. A line is cut at 128 characters.
  A DO WHILE line, its line end included, is under 256 bytes.
  An error in a command file prints "Line n: " and the line, and ends every
  command file.
  HELPDB name gives each command.
:PROMPT
PROMPT text
  Part of @. HELPDB @ gives @ ... PROMPT, HELPDB MENU the picking.
:PUSH
PUSH
  Saves the work area in use, its current record and BOF(). POP gives
  them back. PUSHes nest 4 deep; a fifth gives Stack full.
  The table, its filter and its indexes are not saved.
  PUSH
  SELECT SECONDARY
  POP
:QUIT
QUIT
  Ends every command file, closes the tables of both work areas, and ends
  DB.
:READ
READ
  Edits the pending GETs in turn, from the first. HELPDB KEYS gives the
  keys. READ with no GETs does nothing.
  READ stores each GET in its field or variable, first to last: a text as
  it is, a number read as a number expression, 0 when blank, a logical true
  for T or Y. A field takes the value as REPLACE does, and the record is
  written.
  A wrong value prints its error. The GETs after it are not stored, and no
  field of the record changes.
  Then the GETs are dropped, and the cursor goes to column 0 of the row
  after the last GET's row.
:RECALL
RECALL [scope]
  Clears the deleted mark of each record of the scope that meets its FOR
  condition, and prints "n records recalled". The scope is the current
  record when left out.
  RECALL ALL
:RECNO
RECNO()
  The number of the current record. Past the last record it is the record
  count plus 1. With no table in use it is 0.
:REG
REG key TO text | REG(key)
  REG key TO text sets key in the settings store C:\6502DOS.REG to text.
  The text "" removes key.
  REG(key), the function, is the text of key, "" when key is missing.
  REG GREETING TO "Hello"
  ? REG("GREETING")
:REINDEX
REINDEX
  Builds each open index again from the table, and prints "n records
  indexed" for each. The first index is then the order, and the first
  record of the order is current. A build that fails prints its error and
  closes the indexes.
:RELEASE
RELEASE name [, name ...] | RELEASE ALL
  Drops the memory variables named. RELEASE ALL drops them all. A name with
  no variable gives Variable not found.
  RELEASE WHO, AGE
:REPLACE
REPLACE [scope] field WITH value [, field WITH value ...] [scope]
  Sets each field to its value, left to right, in each record of the scope
  that meets its FOR condition, and prints "n records replaced". The scope
  goes before the fields, after them, or both. It is the current record
  when left out. At most 8 fields.
  An N field takes a number at the field's decimals. A number longer than
  the field gives Number out of range. An L field takes a logical, a C or D
  field text cut to the field's length. A value of another kind gives Type
  mismatch, and a name that is no field Field not found.
  A record that fails part way is left unchanged.
  REPLACE ALL PRICE WITH PRICE * 1.1
:RESTORE
RESTORE SCREEN FROM path
  Puts the cells of a SAVE SCREEN file back on the screen, with .SCR added
  to path when it has no extension. A short file restores the rows it
  holds. The cursor does not move.
  RESTORE SCREEN FROM MAIN
:RETURN
RETURN
  Ends the innermost command file, and its caller goes on. At the prompt
  RETURN does nothing.
:RUN
RUN [command line]
  The same as !. HELPDB ! gives !.
:SAVE
SAVE SCREEN TO path
  Writes the 80 by 60 screen's cells to path, with .SCR added when it has
  no extension: each cell its character then its color, 160 bytes a row,
  with no header. RESTORE SCREEN FROM reads it back.
  SAVE SCREEN TO MAIN
:SAY
SAY expression
  Part of @. HELPDB @ gives @ ... SAY.
:SCOPE
ALL | NEXT n | RECORD n  [FOR condition]
  A scope picks the records a command works on. It has at most one of ALL,
  NEXT n and RECORD n, and a FOR condition, in any order.
  ALL is every record, in the order. NEXT n is n records along the order,
  from the current one. RECORD n is record n alone. FOR keeps the records
  where condition, a logical, is true.
  FOR with none of ALL, NEXT and RECORD is every record.
  With no scope, LIST, LOCATE, COUNT and SUM take every record, and
  DISPLAY, REPLACE, DELETE and RECALL the current record.
  After ALL the place past the last record is current. After NEXT n the
  scope's last record is, or the place past the last when the order ends
  first.
  RECORD n with n not a record gives Record out of range.
  A field named ALL, NEXT, RECORD, FOR or TO is read as the scope word.
  LIST NEXT 10 FOR PAID
:SCREENS
@  SAY  GET  PICTURE  READ  PROMPT  MENU  BOX  CLEAR  CLS  SAVE  RESTORE
  @ ... SAY prints at a place on the screen. @ ... GET adds a pending GET,
  and READ edits the pending GETs. @ ... PROMPT and MENU TO, or MENU(),
  pick from a list. BOX and @ ... TO draw a box. CLEAR blanks the screen
  and drops the GETs. SAVE SCREEN and RESTORE SCREEN keep the screen in a
  file. SET REVERSE shows text in reverse video.
  HELPDB name gives each, and HELPDB KEYS gives READ's keys.
:SELECT
SELECT PRIMARY | SELECT SECONDARY
  Makes that work area the one in use. Each area keeps its table, its
  indexes and its current record. SELECT ends the LOCATE.
  P.name and S.name give a field of the PRIMARY or SECONDARY table from
  either area.
  SELECT SECONDARY
:SET
SET INDEX TO [name [, name ...]]
SET PATH TO [folder [; folder ...]]
SET FILTER TO [condition]
SET REVERSE ON | SET REVERSE OFF
  SET INDEX TO closes the open indexes, then opens each name, with .IDX
  added when it has no extension. The first is the order, and the first
  record of the order is current. SET INDEX TO alone leaves the record
  order. An index that fails to open prints its error, and the names after
  it are not opened.
  SET PATH TO gives the folders where USE, the index names and DO look for
  a name not found, when it has no : or \. SET PATH TO alone clears them.
  SET FILTER TO makes GO TOP, GO BOTTOM, SKIP, the scopes and COPY TO pass
  over the records where condition, a logical, is false. The current record
  stays. GO n does not check it. SET FILTER TO alone clears it, and USE and
  CREATE clear it. Each work area keeps its own filter.
  SET REVERSE ON, or SET REV ON, shows what prints after in reverse video.
  SET REVERSE OFF gives the usual colors back.
  SET INDEX TO NAMES, CITIES
  SET PATH TO C:\DATA; C:\OLD
  SET FILTER TO AGE > 30
:SHELL
SHELL [command line]
  The same as !. HELPDB ! gives !.
:SHOWING
LIST  DISPLAY  ?  COUNT  SUM
  The commands that print records and values.
  HELPDB name gives each command.
:SKIP
SKIP [n]
  Moves n records along the order, 1 when n is left out, back when n is
  negative. It stops past the last record, or at the first record with
  BOF() .T.. SKIP prints "Record n".
  SKIP -2
:STORE
STORE expression TO name | name = expression
  Puts the value in the memory variable name, made when it is new. A name
  is letters, digits and _, starting with a letter; a longer one is cut to
  10 characters.
  At most 64 variables, else Too many variables. The values share 7168
  bytes, else Out of memory, and a text that does not fit releases name.
  name = expression with a space after name runs the command when name is
  a command word or its first 4 letters or more. STORE has no such limit.
  STORE "Smith" TO WHO
:STR
STR(number [, length [, decimals]])
  number as text, at decimals places, right-aligned in length characters.
  length is 10 and decimals 0 when left out. A number longer than length
  comes back whole. length is at most 82 and decimals at most 9, else
  Number out of range.
  ? STR(PRICE, 8, 2)
:SUBSTR
SUBSTR(text, start [, count])
  count characters of text from start, the first character 1. With no
  count it is the rest of text. start is 1 to 255; 0 gives Number out of
  range.
  ? SUBSTR(NAME, 1, 3)
:SUM
SUM expression [, expression ...] [scope] [TO name [, name ...]]
  Adds up each number expression over the records of the scope that meet
  its FOR condition. Prints "n records summed", then each sum on a line.
  At most 8 expressions. The scope is every record when left out. TO stores
  the sums in order, one name for each.
  SUM PRICE, PRICE * QTY FOR PAID TO TOTAL, VALUE
:TABLES
USE  CREATE  DISPLAY STRUCTURE  SELECT  PUSH  POP  COPY  SET
  A table is a .DB file of records, each of the same fields. A field is C
  text, N a number, L a logical or D a date as text.
  DB has two work areas, PRIMARY and SECONDARY, each with a table of its
  own. A command works on the area in use. PUSH and POP save and take back
  the area in use and its record.
  HELPDB name gives each of USE, CREATE, DISPLAY, SELECT, PUSH, POP, COPY
  and SET.
:TRIM
TRIM(text)
  text without its trailing spaces.
  ? TRIM(FIRST) + " " + LAST
:UPPER
UPPER(text)
  text in capitals.
:USE
USE [path [INDEX name [, name ...]]]
  Closes the table in use in the work area, then opens path, with .DB added
  when it has no extension, and each index name, with .IDX added. The first
  index is the order, and the first record of the order is current. An
  index that fails to open prints its error, and the names after it are
  not opened. USE alone only closes the table. USE ends the LOCATE.
  USE PEOPLE INDEX NAMES
:VAL
VAL(text)
  The number text starts with, 0 when it starts with none.
:VARIABLES
STORE  name = value  RELEASE  DISPLAY MEMORY  ACCEPT  INPUT  WAIT
  A memory variable holds a number, a text or a logical. It is made when a
  value is first stored in it. COUNT TO and SUM TO store in variables too.
  A variable keeps its value until RELEASE or QUIT.
  HELPDB name gives each of STORE, RELEASE, DISPLAY, ACCEPT, INPUT and
  WAIT.
:WAIT
WAIT [TO name]
  Prints WAITING and waits for a key. TO stores the key in the memory
  variable name as a text of 1 character, or "" for an extended key.
:ZAP
ZAP
  Removes every record of the table in use, and prints "0 records left".
  It asks nothing first. The open indexes are built again, empty.
